Requirement withdrawal by coding agents
======================================

This package accompanies "Can Coding Agents Let Go? Understanding Behavioral
Residue and Preservation After Requirement Withdrawal." It contains 72 original
programs, three revision conditions and two models (432 sessions). The main
analysis uses the 69 programs with evidence of the added restriction. A second
analysis requires evidence of the earlier behavior in Phase I (62 programs).

Reproduce the results
---------------------
Use Python 3.12. From the repository root:

  python3.12 -m venv .venv
  source .venv/bin/activate
  python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
  python workflow/check_prompts.py
  python reproduce.py --part check
  python reproduce.py --part all --output outputs/run1

The last command rebuilds the numerical tables, manual-review summaries and
quantitative figures. It compares results with the released files and writes
outputs/run1/REPRODUCTION.json. Choose a new output directory for each run.
For the two human reviews alone, use --part manual. These commands use saved
observations and run offline after dependencies are installed.

Where to find the evidence
--------------------------
  metadata/programs.csv    Starting sources, adaptations and cohort membership.
  metadata/sessions.csv    One row per session with its effective grade.
  metadata/variables.txt   Outcome definitions, denominators and table fields.
  programs/                Original Phase I and II programs, prepared sources
                           for 13 adapted programs, and their cleanup patches.
                           For the other 59, Repo_V2 in the program index points
                           directly to the original Phase II bytes used.
  model_inputs/            Exact prompts, original requirements, shared editing
                           instructions and provider tool definitions.
  model_runs/              Per-session plans, API requests and responses, tool
                           activity, final source, outcomes and event logs.
                           records/ gives the execution settings.
  evaluation/              Task A (scheduling) and Task B (allocation) hidden
                           tests, O1/O2 keys, attribution controls and baselines.
  manual_review/           Review criteria, independent and final ratings,
                           sample membership and data behind the matched example.
                           Start with codebook.txt. results/ gives the outcomes.
  analysis/descriptive/    Program- and test-level observations, rates,
                           group distributions, and results by condition.
  analysis/statistics/     Paired observations and complete comparison tables.
  analysis/sensitivity/    Additional specification-conflict comparisons.
  analysis/supplement/     Joint success, recovery, core-test transitions,
                           counts and statistical procedures.
  analysis/figures/        The paper's figures and their sources.
  workflow/                Prompt checking, source preparation, grading,
                           execution and quantitative analysis code.

RR keeps the added rule active. SA asks the model to align code with the supplied
requirements. EW explicitly names the withdrawn rule and asks the model to remove
its restriction. O1 measures expected behavior after withdrawal. O2 checks for
the added restriction with supporting controls. The test and prompt records in
manual_review/ link back to these same experimental inputs.

One session, S0033_SA_openai_r1, needed a ninth turn. Its extra_turn/ folder
contains the additional exchange and effective grade. The first eight exchanges
are in the parent session. The initial result is retained for the turn-limit
sensitivity analysis. The 432 sessions count this program/condition/model once.

Archived paths in the original evidence use the study's execution layout.
metadata/source_map.json resolves inputs used by the analysis. Each final.py
contains the final workspace source. The event journals also name transport
receipts and normalized responses; the actual request/response bodies are in
attempts/. The numerical reproduction uses the saved grades. Executing the
original grader requires the historical Mac environment and is sensitive to
the system date. See metadata/environment.json for versions and NOTICE for
the provenance and reuse of participant materials.
